--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
AFFECTED PROJECTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

vUX library (this repository) — the only project affected. Documentation only; no
code changed and no consumer action is required.

  - doc/resizer.md   new, 315 lines
  - doc/README.md    guides table, "coming next" list, other references
  - ChangeLog.md     one bullet under v4.0.0-beta Added

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Resizer had the most recently changed behaviour in the library and no guide at all.
Two pieces of its API existed only in changelog entries and an example README:
destroy(), and the rule that a handle grows the element in the direction it faces.
doc/README.md listed Resizer among the modules whose guides were "coming next".

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE GUIDE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Follows the structure doc/auto-writer.md established: How it works, Quick start, The
handle, Direction and edges, Bounds, Reading the size, Styling the handle,
Configuration table, Lifecycle, Errors, Recipes, Notes and limits.

Four sections exist because the module has a sharp edge a consumer will otherwise hit
and misread as a bug:

  Direction and edges  States the growth rule, then separates it from position. The
                       module writes a size and never a position, so a left- or
                       top-handled element in normal flow resizes correctly but
                       visibly expands the opposite way. The two flexbox lines that
                       fix it are given rather than left to be worked out.

  Bounds               Leads with 0 meaning *unset* rather than a bound of zero, so a
                       reader writing maxWidth:0 expecting a collapsed element learns
                       otherwise. Also states that these bounds do not know about CSS
                       min-width/max-width on the same element, which the browser
                       applies on top.

  Styling the handle   What resizer.css does and does not provide, the
                       hit-area-versus-visible-line split, and the specificity trap:
                       resizer.css is injected during initialize(), so a bare
                       .resizeHandle rule in the page's own stylesheet ties with it
                       and loses on source order. Both limits of the config `styles`
                       route are stated — it reaches every Resizer on the page, and
                       it does nothing at all when myResizeHandler is set.

  Lifecycle            destroy(), the idempotence of both methods, and what destroy()
                       deliberately does not undo: the inline width/height it wrote,
                       and a position:relative added to a parent that had none.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CAPABILITY GAPS RECORDED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

"Notes and limits" states two gaps plainly rather than omitting them:

  - The module listens for mousedown/mousemove/mouseup only. There is no touch or
    pointer-event support, so handles do not work on a touchscreen.
  - A handle is a plain div with no tabindex and no key handling, so there is no
    keyboard path to resizing. A workaround is suggested — offer another control that
    writes style.width directly — rather than leaving it implicit.

Both are honest descriptions of the module as it stands, not planned work. If either
is to be closed, it is a code change and belongs in its own feature.

